 "answer": "Three stationary designs were run on the same five sleeves. differences b_own 0.431 [0.341, 0.520] t=9.44 | b_agg 0.879 [0.769, 0.990] t=15.57; frozen_z b_own 0.017 [-0.004, 0.038] t=1.62 | b_agg 0.101 [0.064, 0.137] t=5.41; two_way_fe b_own 0.024 [0.012, 0.035] t=3.97 | b_agg absorbed [-, -] t=-. Sign agreement: b_own sign True over 3 readers (size False, span 0.017..0.431), b_agg sign True over 2 readers (the date fixed effects absorb the aggregate channel by construction, so design C cannot vote on it). Pooled verdict after the spec check: unresolved_magnitude_disagreement. Negative own elasticity survived: False. Re-priced for the record: binary_low linear 1538.8 -> per-sleeve - -> aggregate - bp; tilt_step linear 1610.6 -> per-sleeve - -> aggregate - bp; tilt_linear linear 1707.4 -> per-sleeve - -> aggregate - bp. Conclusion: the designs agree on the SIGN of b_own but not on its SIZE (span 0.017..0.431, ratio 25.01, disjoint intervals on [['differences', 'frozen_z'], ['differences', 'two_way_fe']]). That is the signature of two different quantities wearing one name: the difference design prices a session against that session's own turnover change, which moves with the same information that moves |r|, while the frozen-z and date-FE designs price the cross-sectional share and find almost nothing. The bill therefore cannot be built from any of them -- the family's single exponent stays a PRICE without a bill, and E234's per-sleeve sum is not a substitute.",
